  tail ratio         1.2064, cluster-robust CI [1.0135, 1.4372], p=0.0165

Reporting the fourth as a result while the first three were also tried is a
multiplicity problem, and it is MY multiplicity: the tail ratio was constructed
after seeing that the calibration ratio and the PIT disagreed. It is the fourth
look at one panel, not a pre-stated test.

  raw p                      0.0165
  Bonferroni over the family 0.066
  -> NOT significant at 5% family-wise

Recorded status: SUGGESTIVE. The thin-tails reading is the best available
explanation of why the calibration ratio (0.733) and the PIT disagree, and it is
not established.

WHAT IS ROBUST
  seeds        the CI lower bound excludes 1.0 in 8 of 8 bootstrap seeds,
               range 1.0125-1.0179. Thin, but not seed-dependent.
  single names dropping the largest |z| leaves 1.1904; dropping the four largest
               leaves 1.1233. Not one ticker.

THE STRONGEST COMPONENT IS THE EXCEEDANCE COUNTS, not the ratio
  |z| > 2.5    11 observed vs 2.21 expected, over 7 distinct 10-day blocks
  |z| > 3      7 observed vs 0.5 expected, over 7 distinct days and 5 blocks
  |z| > 4      2 observed vs 0.01 expected
They are the strongest because they need no scale estimate and no bootstrap: a
14x excess at |z|>3 is a count against a binomial, and the spread across 5-7
blocks is a fact about the calendar rather than a modelling choice.

WHAT THE EXCEEDANCES DO RULE OUT
  - one bad market day as the whole explanation. Seven exceedances on seven
    distinct dates in five distinct blocks is not a single event, which is the
    obvious deflationary reading of a clustered panel and it does not hold.
  - a pure SCALE error as the whole explanation. MAD-scale(z)=1.1052 with a
    cluster-robust CI of [0.9307, 1.4139], which COVERS 1.0 -- the body is
    already consistent with calibrated. Widening everything cannot be the fix.

WHAT THEY DO NOT RULE OUT
  - that this is a property of the TARGET, not a defect of this forecaster.
    Earnings announcements carry genuine jump risk; any normal-tailed model of a
    5-day post-announcement window would show the same excess. Nothing here
    separates "M.A.P. underestimates tails" from "5-day earnings returns have
    fat tails and a Gaussian mixture cannot represent them".
  - that rho=0.276 understates the dependence. If items correlate across
    adjacent blocks as well as within them, every interval above is still too
    narrow and 0.066 is still too small.
  - the mechanism. Four of the seven exceedances are small, high-volatility
    names (CELH, INTC, IONQ x2, CORZ). Whether the tail excess is concentrated
    in a subpopulation is untested, and n=178 cannot test it.

This record exists so that a later confirmation is a replication rather than a
rediscovery, and so that the 0.0165 is never quoted without the 0.066 beside it.

